{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013dvkVNbPTBNWKe5hVk3EYS","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# The 495 development cycle\n2\t\n3\tThis directory holds the way a change to 495 is made: the six steps, the review and landing rules,\n4\tthe story format, and the tool that drives them all. It says nothing about the product. What 495 is,\n5\twhat it still has to do and what it has proved live in `specs/`.\n6\t\n7\tThe cycle depends on two outside packages, both development dependencies of the repository: Stryker,\n8\twhich runs the mutation (`D-89`), and the YAML reader Pi already installs, which reads the defect\n9\tregistry. On 2026-09-28 it replaced the bigpowers skills and the script that chained them, after\n10\tmeasurement: a 3,900-line story cost 5 hours of agent time and $88, and the fix cycle for its four\n11\tdefects 7 hours and $105, stopped at a cap of five review rounds. More than half of that time went\n12\tto reviewers who replayed mutations by hand and found another corner of the state machine at every\n13\tround; per story, a thousand lines of records were written by hand and read again by every session;\n14\tacross all of `specs/`, a single tracking file was read by a check.\n15\t\n16\t## A dedicated cycle, and a laboratory\n17\t\n18\t495 drives a software change through gated, evidence-backed phases, but it cannot yet drive its own.\n19\tUntil 495 can be used to develop 495, its changes go through this dedicated cycle, with its own tool\n20\tand Claude Code sessions in place of 495's confined Pi worker.\n21\t\n22\tThe cycle is also a laboratory. Each change it carries tries out ways of driving a change an agent\n23\tmakes, and shows which hold and which fail under real work and real cost:\n24\t- a story written before the code;\n25\t- a red seen on the announced assertion;\n26\t- two independent reviewers bounded to two rounds;\n27\t- a registry for what review leaves;\n28\t- an acceptance run with a negative control;\n29\t- an independent session that accepts in the owner's place.\n30\t\n31\tA practice that holds is a candidate for 495's own phases. One that fails is dropped together with\n32\tthe measurement that sank it, so that 495 does not inherit it. The cycle itself came out of such a\n33\tmeasurement. The decisions that shape it are recorded in `specs/adr/`, like those of the product.\n34\t\n35\t## How the cycle differs from 495\n36\t\n37\tThe cycle and 495 answer the same question, how to accept a change an agent made, and they do not\n38\tanswer it the same way. Each difference below is one of three kinds:\n39\t- **interim**: it goes away once 495 drives its own changes;\n40\t- **under trial**: a different choice that the laboratory weighs against 495's;\n41\t- **deliberate**: the cycle keeps it whatever 495 does.\n42\t\n43\t495's side is stated by the text that holds it, and only cited here.\n44\t\n45\t- **Who writes the tests: under trial.**\n46\t  - The cycle: the session that writes the code writes its tests first, and the tool replays the\n47\t    test-only commit to see it fail.\n48\t  - 495: a separate preparation intervention writes the tests before any candidate exists, and the\n49\t    kernel judges them on the bare reference and freezes them with the protocol at G2\n50\t    (`src/application/phases/prepare.ts`, `D-14`).\n51\t  - At stake: the separation keeps tests from being cut to fit the code. It costs a frozen test that\n52\t    no implementation can satisfy, which today stops the change (`specs/bugs/registry.yaml`).\n53\t- **Who reviews: under trial.**\n54\t  - The cycle: every story gets two model reviewers, for two rounds, and a promise the code does not\n55\t    keep blocks it.\n56\t  - 495: a review is a role the frozen protocol may require (`required_reviews`, empty by default).\n57\t    When one is required, a rejection or a blocking finding fails G5 (`src/domain/gates/g5.ts`).\n58\t  - At stake: whether systematic review earns what it costs.\n59\t- **Who accepts: under trial.**\n60\t  - The cycle: the owner accepts or names a gap. In an unattended run, a fresh model session decides\n61\t    in the owner's place, and the record says `origine: automate`.\n62\t  - 495: G5 combines evidence, reviews and human decisions deterministically against the frozen\n63\t    protocol. Acceptance follows from G5 by default, or from an IH-10 decision when the policy asks\n64\t    for one. A model call never yields a human decision (`ADR-014`), and only a human undoes what a\n65\t    human decided (`D-70`).\n66\t  - At stake: the cycle lets a model stand in for the owner, which 495 refuses by design. The\n67\t    laboratory measures what that substitution gets right and what it lets through.\n68\t- **Where a human answer comes from: interim.**\n69\t  - The cycle: whoever types `accepte` or `ecart`.\n70\t  - 495: an answer counts only through a Pi dialogue whose provenance the host vouches for, and never\n71\t    through JSON or print mode (`ADR-014`, `D-08`, `D-12`).\n72\t- **Where the agent runs: interim.**\n73\t  - The cycle: Claude Code sessions with the whole repository in reach.\n74\t  - 495: a confined Pi worker that cannot reach the normative storage (`ADR-004`).\n75\t- **Where the checks run: deliberate.**\n76\t  - The cycle: checks run unconfined, because this repository's suite qualifies Seatbelt itself and\n77\t    sandboxes do not nest.\n78\t  - 495: checks run under explicit sandbox profiles and refuse rather than run unconfined\n79\t    (`ADR-013`).\n80\t- **What makes a check an authority: interim.**\n81\t  - The cycle: Preflight is trusted as it stands.\n82\t  - 495: every check used as an authority is qualified first by a positive, a targeted negative and,\n83\t    when it has a runner, an incident case (`specs/amont/conception-verification.md` §2.1).\n84\t- **What the record is: interim.**\n85\t  - The cycle: an append-only journal of JSON lines beside 495's object store, exported at landing.\n86\t  - 495: a SQLite ledger whose events form a digest chain, with the same object store (`ADR-005`).\n87\t\n88\t## The six steps\n89\t\n90\tA story starts on a branch from `main`, on a green Preflight, and comes back to it in one commit.\n91\t\n92\t1. **The story.** Written with the owner in the format of `format-de-story.md`: what the reader\n93\t   gains, the promises as scenarios, security, the tasks, what is out of scope. Each task gives the\n94\t   command that holds it, written red because it names a test that does not exist yet, the test and\n95\t   its assertion in the words of the story, and what the code does today that makes it fail. What\n96\t   needs a hand is written as such. `scripts/check-story-format.ts` refuses a story that misses this\n97\t   shape. A story that carries a verification companion (`format-de-story.md` § Compagnon de\n98\t   vérification) has a preparation, a sub-step of the story the tool examines before any red-green\n99\t   session: an incomplete companion, an undetermined exploration or a counterexample of an adopted\n100\t   model sends the story back to its preparation, naming the trace, without spending a red-green\n101\t   attempt. A story reopened on an unchanged preparation is a code correction; a changed model or\n102\t   promise withdraws the previous preparation, which is examined again.\n103\t2. **Red-green.** Task by task: the test first, its red seen on the announced assertion, a test-only\n104\t   commit, then the code and a green commit. A missing file, an import or type error, or a red\n105\t   obtained by setting code aside is not that red. The tool replays each test-only commit of the\n106\t   pass in a detached tree and reads which tests fail, not only the exit code. A test that a later\n107\t   step adds, already green because the code keeps the promise, is not a commit of the pass. The step\n108\t   ends on a green Preflight.\n109\t3. **Self-review.** A review of the branch diff against the standards of `CONVENTIONS.md`, with the\n110\t   checklist of `prompts/autocontrole.md`: scope kept, dead code, types, one test per function, a\n111\t   single responsibility, unique names. What it finds is fixed on the branch before review.\n112\t4. **Review.** Two fresh reviewers, with no shared context, in parallel, each in its own copy of the\n113\t   tree. The first round reviews the branch against `main`; the second, the diff since the reviewed\n114\t   revision and the findings already handled. There is no third round.\n115\t5. **The acceptance run.** A real execution: the extension loaded from `dist/` into a real Pi, a real\n116\t   model or a scripted agent declared as such, a campaign carried to its verdict, then the record\n117\t   read back from SQLite and the object store. A negative control goes with the green campaign: the\n118\t   same setup deprived of what the story adds, and the refusal it produces. A list backed by tests is\n119\t   not an acceptance run. When the branch changes a file under `src/application/stacks/`,\n120\t   `src/adapters/stacks/`, `src/adapters/execution/`, `src/adapters/sandbox/` or `src/domain/gates/`\n121\t   (a technology, a control or the executor; the list lives in `cycle/src/campagnes-exigees.ts`), the\n122\t   tool itself plays the two reference campaigns of `cycle/campagnes/` (`npm run campagne -- npm` and\n123\t   `-- maven`), which were not cut to fit it, at the head of the branch, before the acceptance\n124\t   session, and keeps each verdict in the record with its revision (`D-89`); they also run before\n125\t   every release. The owner accepts, or names the gap. On such a branch the tool refuses the\n126\t   agreement, the owner's or the arbitration's, naming the campaign at fault, while one is missing,\n127\t   failed or played at another revision than the head: a campaign the acceptance session says it\n128\t   played does not count.\n129\t6. **Landing.** The branch reaches `main` as one squashed commit, whose message states the resulting\n130\t   behaviour, in English, on one line. The branch is kept: the record cites its commits. The story's\n131\t   record is exported under `specs/verifications/<story>/`, the story becomes `versée`, and nothing\n132\t   is pushed: pushing is the owner's.\n133\t\n134\tA gap found at the acceptance run goes back to step 2 for the gap alone, then to one review round on\n135\tits diff, then to the acceptance run.\n136\t\n137\t## Review\n138\t\n139\tWhat reviewers check: the promises of the story and its security section, and nothing else. For\n140\teach scenario: does the code keep it, and does a test hold it, one that a one-line mutation of the\n141\tline keeping the promise must make fail. A promise the code does not keep is blocking; a promise no\n142\ttest holds is to be fixed. Conventions, design and smells belong to the self-review, which comes\n143\tfirst. For the promises, the prompt offers the reviewers no scenario, state or interleaving of its\n144\town invention: a defect on a path no promise covers will be found by the acceptance run, by use, or\n145\tby the story that makes it a promise.\n146\t\n147\tThe security section is reviewed by attacking it (`specs/adr/D-89`): for every guarantee of that\n148\tsection, each reviewer looks for a concrete path, through the public entries the branch exposes, that\n149\tobtains what the guarantee refuses. A path found is a bypass, marked as such in the finding with the\n150\tguarantee it gets around. A bypass is blocking and holds the gate whatever its location; it never\n151\tgoes to the registry, and after the last round it is put to the owner as a promise the code does not\n152\tkeep. The answer to a round cannot class it elsewhere: a bypass it does not answer as fixed by a\n153\tcommit it made, whether contested, registered or claimed fixed without one, holds the next round's\n154\tgate as it stands. One it answers as fixed holds that gate too, until each reviewer of the next round\n155\thas replayed its path on the head of the branch and found it closed: a commit alone does not show\n156\twhat it fixes. A bypass the last round sends back to step 2 holds, in the same way, the gate of the\n157\tround that follows the reopening. A review step relaunched after a block resumes at the round that\n158\tfollows the last one answered, with the bypasses that answer left open or claimed fixed.\n159\t\n160\tA finding is located before it is handled. A defect the branch introduces or makes reachable belongs\n161\tto it, even when the faulty line predates it; a defect it neither introduces nor makes reachable goes\n162\tto the registry and does not hold the review back.\n163\t\n164\tThe answer to a round fixes what is blocking or to be fixed. A suggestion whose fix adds no behaviour\n165\tis fixed in the round; a suggestion whose fix would add a refusal, a state or a mechanism goes to the\n166\tregistry. A fix that adds a mechanism is designed before it is put in: where the state is born, who\n167\treads it, how many entries lead to it; removing a second entry is better than keeping it. A finding\n168\tthat touches only text is fixed without being reviewed by fresh reviewers: the coordinator checks the\n169\tfix against its finding.\n170\t\n171\tThe gate closes without a percentage. After the second round, what remains goes to the registry\n172\t`specs/bugs/registry.yaml`, named as introduced by the branch when it is, except a promise the code\n173\tdoes not keep, a bypass of the security section included, which is put to the owner: they decide\n174\tbetween landing and a fix, which goes back through step 2 and one round on its diff.\n175\t\n176\tRecords do not move during review. The revision, timestamps and test count of a record are set when\n177\tthe gate passes. A record lagging behind the reviewed revision is not a finding, any more than the\n178\tsubject of a branch commit, which never reaches `main`.\n179\t\n180\t## Preflight and defects found along the way\n181\t\n182\tPreflight is `npm run check`. It is green before every step, and before every commit that touches\n183\t`src/`, `test/`, `scripts/`, `bench/`, `contracts/`, `README.md`, `NOTICE`, `LICENSE`,\n184\t`package.json`, `package-lock.json`, a `tsconfig*.json` or `biome.json`. A story under\n185\t`specs/stories/` calls for `npm run lint:story-format`; an archive file calls for the check that\n186\treads it. A green Preflight holds as long as no file of the first list has changed: cite its\n187\trevision and time rather than run it again. Only a run under Node 24 counts.\n188\t\n189\tIn a run of the tool, only the tool runs Preflight: a session runs what its change touches — the tests\n190\tconcerned, the typecheck, the lint — and hands its output back. After the red-green, the self-review\n191\tand an answer to a review round, a red Preflight goes to a correction session with the failures the tool\n192\tread, then runs again, at most twice before the step blocks. The base of a story and the squashed\n193\tlanding have no session behind them: a red Preflight there blocks at once, and so does one after a\n194\trefactoring.\n195\t\n196\tA reproducible failure met along the way is a defect found, never background noise. It is fixed at\n197\tonce, in its own commit, with its regression test, when the fix adds no behaviour; otherwise it gets\n198\tan entry in `specs/bugs/registry.yaml` and its own cycle. It is recorded without a fix only if its\n199\treproduction fails after a good-faith attempt. \"Already there before\", \"unrelated to the session\",\n200\t\"out of scope\" are not answers.\n201\t\n202\t## Git and commits\n203\t\n204\tOne branch per story or per fix cycle. Commit message: `<type>: <description>`, one line, in English,\n205\twhatever the language of the session or of the file changed; the types are `feat`, `fix`,\n206\t`refactor`, `docs`, `test`, `chore`, `perf`, `ci`. The message states the resulting behaviour, never\n207\tthe process that produced it: no work-package reference, no review round, no attribution, no\n208\t`Co-Authored-By`. Nothing is pushed by an automaton; `gh` for every GitHub operation, never the API\n209\tdirectly; no issue is created by an automaton.\n210\t\n211\t## What is written by hand, and what is observed\n212\t\n213\tBy hand: a product decision in `specs/adr/`, the order of the work in `specs/plan.yaml`, where\n214\tthe owner marks `prete: oui` the epics that run without them, and, for every epic added to the plan,\n215\tits dossier in `specs/epics/`, which the plan entry cites as `source:` and the session that writes\n216\tits stories reads. The story and the registry entry are\n217\twritten by hand when the owner drives the story; in a run without them, a session writes them under\n218\tthe rules of this file. Everything else is observed and recorded by the tool at the moment it\n219\tobserves it: a red and its message, a Preflight and its revision, a review round and its findings,\n220\tthe acceptance run and the owner's agreement, the landing. Evidence is written once, in the record of\n221\tthe step that produced it; elsewhere it is cited. A copy drifts from the code it describes.\n222\t\n223\t## The cycle without the owner\n224\t\n225\t`npm run cycle -- suite` runs, one after the other, the epics that `specs/plan.yaml` marks\n226\t`prete: oui`, in the order of the plan. For each: the next story of the plan is written by a session\n227\twhen none is listed to do, it is driven to landing as above, the plan marks it landed, and the epic\n228\tbecomes `versé` when a session finds that its stories deliver its purpose. The run starts from `main`\n229\twith a clean tree and stops at the first block, saying why; it never pushes.\n230\t`npm run cycle -- <story> auto` does the same for a single story.\n231\t\n232\tThree of the owner's answers are delegated, each an act recorded in the record:\n233\t\n234\t- **Agreement after the acceptance run.** A fresh session, which drove neither the acceptance run\n235\t  nor the review, reads the story, the report of the acceptance run and the defects the branch\n236\t  records in the registry, then decides `accepte` or `ecart` (`prompts/arbitrage.md`). For each\n237\t  defect the branch records, it retains a severity and says why: the registry carries it, the record\n238\t  keeps it beside the severity the branch wrote, and the tool stops, naming the defect, when one has\n239\t  none. The tool reads the registry with a YAML reader, so it sees an entry whatever valid YAML form\n240\t  it is written in, and stops, naming the registry, when the reader does not read it or the branch\n241\t  adds an item that is not a defect with an identifier. The owner, when the story waits for them,\n242\t  sees those defects with their severity in the question. A gap is a\n243\t  written promise or a security guarantee not kept, a case the previous code stopped and the branch\n244\t  lets through, or a defect of the branch that weakens a guarantee of the story; otherwise the\n245\t  agreement carries a note naming what remains in the registry and what the acceptance run did not\n246\t  exercise. A defect the arbitration finds and the registry does not carry, it records there and\n247\t  commits; the tool stops if it touches another file or leaves the tree modified. A product question\n248\t  the story does not settle is settled by the behaviour that stops. The decision is recorded with\n249\t  `origine: automate`.\n250\t- **The gap.** Whether the arbitration names it or the acceptance run reopens it, a session writes it\n251\t  into the story (a scenario, the security sentence, a task whose red is checked in the code) and\n252\t  commits it before red-green starts again: without a written promise, review cannot judge the fix.\n253\t  The tool reads the story again, requires that it gained a scenario or a task and that the tree is\n254\t  clean.\n255\t- **A promise review did not get kept.** It goes back to red-green: it was not the owner's to waive.\n256\t\n257\t**Registry defects** are fixed too, at the right time (`D-73`): at the end of each epic, before the\n258\tnext one, those of medium or high severity, whether the suite drove the epic or `cycle <story> auto`\n259\tlanded its last story; at the end of the run, the low ones. A session picks the first open defect\n260\tthat needs no product decision, writes its fix story under epic `e28` (which the plan never marks\n261\tready) citing the registry entry, and the tool drives it through the six steps; at landing, the\n262\tentry moves from the registry to `specs/bugs/registry-fixed.yaml`, marked fixed at the landed\n263\trevision. The defects the session sets aside because they need the owner are named at the end of the\n264\trun, with the reason, without stopping it. One phase fixes at most `CYCLE_495_DEFAUTS_MAX` defects\n265\t(5 by default); `npm run cycle -- defauts [severity]` runs that phase alone.\n266\t\n267\tThe run stops, and hands back control, when a story has been sent back to red-green three times,\n268\twhen it has spent more than `CYCLE_495_PLAFOND_USD` ($80 by default), when a session does not return\n269\tits output, or when a step blocks: a defect of the tool itself, a test that cannot be red, a red\n270\tPreflight. Writing a story stops the same way on what it cannot write without choosing in the owner's\n271\tplace (`bloque`, with the choice named), and an epic with no possible first story stays the owner's.\n272\t\n273\t## Refactorings\n274\t\n275\tA refactoring changes no behaviour: same events, same artifacts, same verdicts, same refusals, same\n276\tcontracts, same texts meant for the owner or the model. It has no promise to review, no red to see,\n277\tnothing to show at an acceptance run; so it does not go through the six steps, and takes the short\n278\tpath of `D-80`. What changes a behaviour, even slightly, is a story.\n279\t\n280\tRefactorings are written in `specs/reprises.md`, one section per refactoring, in the order they are\n281\tdone. `npm run cycle -- reprises` drives them one after the other, from `main` and a clean tree:\n282\t\n283\t1. Preflight is green on `main`, after rebuilding `dist/`, and the tool reads its test count.\n284\t2. A session does the refactoring on the branch `reprise-<id>` (`prompts/reprise.md`). It may set it\n285\t   aside, with the reason, when the refactoring would change a behaviour or the code contradicts it:\n286\t   the tool writes that into the list, commits, and moves to the next one.\n287\t3. The tool checks, without trusting the session: at least one commit, a clean tree, the list\n288\t   untouched, no assertion line of `test/` removed without coming back identical, Preflight green\n289\t   after rebuilding `dist/`, and no fewer tests than before.\n290\t4. A fresh session reviews the diff in a detached copy (`prompts/reprise-relecture.md`) and says\n291\t   whether a behaviour changes or the diff overflows the refactoring. There is no second round.\n292\t5. The branch reaches `main` in one commit, which marks the refactoring `versée` in the list; the\n293\t   branch is deleted, the log under `~/.495/cycle/<id>/` keeps the transcripts.\n294\t\n295\tThe run stops, and hands back control, at the first check that fails or at a review that sees a\n296\tchange: the branch stays checked out so it can be read, `main` and the list do not move. To resume,\n297\tgo back to `main` (`git checkout main`): the next run starts the same refactoring from a fresh branch,\n298\tor it is set aside by hand in the list with the reason. When review refuses what the refactoring\n299\titself asks for, running again replays the same refusal: reduce the refactoring to its constant part,\n300\tsaying in `Limite` what it does not do, and the rest goes to the registry or to a story.\n301\t`CYCLE_495_REPRISES_MAX` caps the number of refactorings in one run. Nothing is pushed.\n302\t\n303\t## The tool\n304\t\n305\t`npm run cycle -- <story>` drives the remaining steps, in order, until a step needs the owner or\n306\tblocks; `npm run cycle -- <story> etat` says where it stands;\n307\t`npm run cycle -- <story> suivre` follows, from another terminal, the story that is running;\n308\t`npm run cycle -- suite` runs the epics marked ready, without the owner;\n309\t`npm run cycle -- defauts [severity]` fixes the open defects of the registry, without running an epic;\n310\t`npm run cycle -- reprises` drives the behaviour-preserving refactorings of `specs/reprises.md`;\n311\t`npm run cycle -- <story> auto` drives a single story the same way;\n312\t`npm run cycle -- <story> accepte [note]` records the agreement after the acceptance run;\n313\t`npm run cycle -- <story> ecart \"<what is missing>\"` sends it back to red-green for the named gap,\n314\twith a single review round on its diff. The record lives under `~/.495/cycle/<story>/` during the\n315\tstory (`CYCLE_495_DIR` moves it).\n316\t\n317\tWhile it runs, the command shows in the terminal where the story stands: each step opens with its\n318\tplace among the six, with the time and cost already spent; each session prints one line per agent\n319\ttext, tool call, commit, test total or failed call, prefixed with its name when two reviewers run\n320\ttogether; each check says when it starts and how it ends; each step closes on its outcome, duration,\n321\tcost and commits. The terminal title names the current step and how long nothing has been printed. A\n322\tsound and a notification say that a step waits for the owner, that it blocks or that the story has\n323\tlanded (`CYCLE_495_SON` changes the sound). The same lines go to `en-direct.log`, in the story's\n324\tdirectory, which `npm run cycle -- <story> suivre` follows from another terminal until Ctrl-C; a new\n325\trun empties that file. Nothing in this display is evidence: the log and the kept transcripts are.\n326\t\n327\tThe tool depends on 495 and never the reverse. It takes from the kernel the object store, where the\n328\ttranscripts, check outputs and reviewer reports go; the check executor and its report readers,\n329\tthrough which Preflight, each task's command and the replay of each red pass; and the shape of\n330\tevidence. Checks run unconfined, and each piece of evidence says so: this repository's suite\n331\tqualifies Seatbelt itself, and sandboxes do not nest.\n332\t\n333\tThe steps that need a model run in Claude Code sessions, one per step, started with no background\n334\ttask and no possible question, with a structured output the tool reads: red-green, self-review, the\n335\ttwo reviewers of each round in parallel in their detached trees, the answer, the acceptance run, the\n336\tlanding message. Their prompts are in `prompts/`. The story itself is written with the owner, in an\n337\tordinary session.\n338\t\n339\tWhat the tool checks itself, without trusting the session: each test-only commit of the pass,\n340\treplayed in a detached tree, fails on a test it reads; each task command passes at the branch head;\n341\tPreflight is green at the cited revision; review stops at two rounds; the branch reaches `main` in\n342\tone commit; both reference campaigns are green at the head the agreement accepts, when the branch\n343\trequires them, which it judges from the base it recorded when the story's branch started; no session\n344\twrites the story's record, and what one adds or removes there while it runs is taken back out and\n345\tblocks its step; the record is exported at landing. A session that forges what the tool reads back\n346\tbehind its back — the record, the repository's git configuration, a detached process writing after\n347\tthese checks — stays outside them while the sessions run unconfined.\n348\t\n349\tBefore the first review round, the tool mutates the lines the branch introduces in the TypeScript\n350\tsources of `src/`, `cycle/src/` and `scripts/`, with Stryker and the test files the tasks' commands\n351\tname, in a detached tree removed afterwards; it keeps the report in the record and gives the reviewers\n352\tthe mutants that survive (`D-89`). A mutation that fails or runs past its budget\n353\t(`CYCLE_495_MUTATION_MIN`, 30 minutes by default) does not hold the round back: the reviewers are told\n354\tit did not complete, and why.\n355\t\n356\tWhat is still missing: 495's confined Pi worker in place of the Claude Code sessions.\n357\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7f1f760d-d955-46ef-8d06-c9ab8889d2a2","uuid":"4b494cc7-0296-43d8-bd13-94cb633393a0","timestamp":"2026-10-10T14:31:07.406Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/jeanjerome/Projets/495-pi-package/cycle/README.md","content":"# The 495 development cycle\n\nThis directory holds the way a change to 495 is made: the six steps, the review and landing rules,\nthe story format, and the tool that drives them all. It says nothing about the product. What 495 is,\nwhat it still has to do and what it has proved live in `specs/`.\n\nThe cycle depends on two outside packages, both development dependencies of the repository: Stryker,\nwhich runs the mutation (`D-89`), and the YAML reader Pi already installs, which reads the defect\nregistry. On 2026-09-28 it replaced the bigpowers skills and the script that chained them, after\nmeasurement: a 3,900-line story cost 5 hours of agent time and $88, and the fix cycle for its four\ndefects 7 hours and $105, stopped at a cap of five review rounds. More than half of that time went\nto reviewers who replayed mutations by hand and found another corner of the state machine at every\nround; per story, a thousand lines of records were written by hand and read again by every session;\nacross all of `specs/`, a single tracking file was read by a check.\n\n## A dedicated cycle, and a laboratory\n\n495 drives a software change through gated, evidence-backed phases, but it cannot yet drive its own.\nUntil 495 can be used to develop 495, its changes go through this dedicated cycle, with its own tool\nand Claude Code sessions in place of 495's confined Pi worker.\n\nThe cycle is also a laboratory. Each change it carries tries out ways of driving a change an agent\nmakes, and shows which hold and which fail under real work and real cost:\n- a story written before the code;\n- a red seen on the announced assertion;\n- two independent reviewers bounded to two rounds;\n- a registry for what review leaves;\n- an acceptance run with a negative control;\n- an independent session that accepts in the owner's place.\n\nA practice that holds is a candidate for 495's own phases. One that fails is dropped together with\nthe measurement that sank it, so that 495 does not inherit it. The cycle itself came out of such a\nmeasurement. The decisions that shape it are recorded in `specs/adr/`, like those of the product.\n\n## How the cycle differs from 495\n\nThe cycle and 495 answer the same question, how to accept a change an agent made, and they do not\nanswer it the same way. Each difference below is one of three kinds:\n- **interim**: it goes away once 495 drives its own changes;\n- **under trial**: a different choice that the laboratory weighs against 495's;\n- **deliberate**: the cycle keeps it whatever 495 does.\n\n495's side is stated by the text that holds it, and only cited here.\n\n- **Who writes the tests: under trial.**\n  - The cycle: the session that writes the code writes its tests first, and the tool replays the\n    test-only commit to see it fail.\n  - 495: a separate preparation intervention writes the tests before any candidate exists, and the\n    kernel judges them on the bare reference and freezes them with the protocol at G2\n    (`src/application/phases/prepare.ts`, `D-14`).\n  - At stake: the separation keeps tests from being cut to fit the code. It costs a frozen test that\n    no implementation can satisfy, which today stops the change (`specs/bugs/registry.yaml`).\n- **Who reviews: under trial.**\n  - The cycle: every story gets two model reviewers, for two rounds, and a promise the code does not\n    keep blocks it.\n  - 495: a review is a role the frozen protocol may require (`required_reviews`, empty by default).\n    When one is required, a rejection or a blocking finding fails G5 (`src/domain/gates/g5.ts`).\n  - At stake: whether systematic review earns what it costs.\n- **Who accepts: under trial.**\n  - The cycle: the owner accepts or names a gap. In an unattended run, a fresh model session decides\n    in the owner's place, and the record says `origine: automate`.\n  - 495: G5 combines evidence, reviews and human decisions deterministically against the frozen\n    protocol. Acceptance follows from G5 by default, or from an IH-10 decision when the policy asks\n    for one. A model call never yields a human decision (`ADR-014`), and only a human undoes what a\n    human decided (`D-70`).\n  - At stake: the cycle lets a model stand in for the owner, which 495 refuses by design. The\n    laboratory measures what that substitution gets right and what it lets through.\n- **Where a human answer comes from: interim.**\n  - The cycle: whoever types `accepte` or `ecart`.\n  - 495: an answer counts only through a Pi dialogue whose provenance the host vouches for, and never\n    through JSON or print mode (`ADR-014`, `D-08`, `D-12`).\n- **Where the agent runs: interim.**\n  - The cycle: Claude Code sessions with the whole repository in reach.\n  - 495: a confined Pi worker that cannot reach the normative storage (`ADR-004`).\n- **Where the checks run: deliberate.**\n  - The cycle: checks run unconfined, because this repository's suite qualifies Seatbelt itself and\n    sandboxes do not nest.\n  - 495: checks run under explicit sandbox profiles and refuse rather than run unconfined\n    (`ADR-013`).\n- **What makes a check an authority: interim.**\n  - The cycle: Preflight is trusted as it stands.\n  - 495: every check used as an authority is qualified first by a positive, a targeted negative and,\n    when it has a runner, an incident case (`specs/amont/conception-verification.md` §2.1).\n- **What the record is: interim.**\n  - The cycle: an append-only journal of JSON lines beside 495's object store, exported at landing.\n  - 495: a SQLite ledger whose events form a digest chain, with the same object store (`ADR-005`).\n\n## The six steps\n\nA story starts on a branch from `main`, on a green Preflight, and comes back to it in one commit.\n\n1. **The story.** Written with the owner in the format of `format-de-story.md`: what the reader\n   gains, the promises as scenarios, security, the tasks, what is out of scope. Each task gives the\n   command that holds it, written red because it names a test that does not exist yet, the test and\n   its assertion in the words of the story, and what the code does today that makes it fail. What\n   needs a hand is written as such. `scripts/check-story-format.ts` refuses a story that misses this\n   shape. A story that carries a verification companion (`format-de-story.md` § Compagnon de\n   vérification) has a preparation, a sub-step of the story the tool examines before any red-green\n   session: an incomplete companion, an undetermined exploration or a counterexample of an adopted\n   model sends the story back to its preparation, naming the trace, without spending a red-green\n   attempt. A story reopened on an unchanged preparation is a code correction; a changed model or\n   promise withdraws the previous preparation, which is examined again.\n2. **Red-green.** Task by task: the test first, its red seen on the announced assertion, a test-only\n   commit, then the code and a green commit. A missing file, an import or type error, or a red\n   obtained by setting code aside is not that red. The tool replays each test-only commit of the\n   pass in a detached tree and reads which tests fail, not only the exit code. A test that a later\n   step adds, already green because the code keeps the promise, is not a commit of the pass. The step\n   ends on a green Preflight.\n3. **Self-review.** A review of the branch diff against the standards of `CONVENTIONS.md`, with the\n   checklist of `prompts/autocontrole.md`: scope kept, dead code, types, one test per function, a\n   single responsibility, unique names. What it finds is fixed on the branch before review.\n4. **Review.** Two fresh reviewers, with no shared context, in parallel, each in its own copy of the\n   tree. The first round reviews the branch against `main`; the second, the diff since the reviewed\n   revision and the findings already handled. There is no third round.\n5. **The acceptance run.** A real execution: the extension loaded from `dist/` into a real Pi, a real\n   model or a scripted agent declared as such, a campaign carried to its verdict, then the record\n   read back from SQLite and the object store. A negative control goes with the green campaign: the\n   same setup deprived of what the story adds, and the refusal it produces. A list backed by tests is\n   not an acceptance run. When the branch changes a file under `src/application/stacks/`,\n   `src/adapters/stacks/`, `src/adapters/execution/`, `src/adapters/sandbox/` or `src/domain/gates/`\n   (a technology, a control or the executor; the list lives in `cycle/src/campagnes-exigees.ts`), the\n   tool itself plays the two reference campaigns of `cycle/campagnes/` (`npm run campagne -- npm` and\n   `-- maven`), which were not cut to fit it, at the head of the branch, before the acceptance\n   session, and keeps each verdict in the record with its revision (`D-89`); they also run before\n   every release. The owner accepts, or names the gap. On such a branch the tool refuses the\n   agreement, the owner's or the arbitration's, naming the campaign at fault, while one is missing,\n   failed or played at another revision than the head: a campaign the acceptance session says it\n   played does not count.\n6. **Landing.** The branch reaches `main` as one squashed commit, whose message states the resulting\n   behaviour, in English, on one line. The branch is kept: the record cites its commits. The story's\n   record is exported under `specs/verifications/<story>/`, the story becomes `versée`, and nothing\n   is pushed: pushing is the owner's.\n\nA gap found at the acceptance run goes back to step 2 for the gap alone, then to one review round on\nits diff, then to the acceptance run.\n\n## Review\n\nWhat reviewers check: the promises of the story and its security section, and nothing else. For\neach scenario: does the code keep it, and does a test hold it, one that a one-line mutation of the\nline keeping the promise must make fail. A promise the code does not keep is blocking; a promise no\ntest holds is to be fixed. Conventions, design and smells belong to the self-review, which comes\nfirst. For the promises, the prompt offers the reviewers no scenario, state or interleaving of its\nown invention: a defect on a path no promise covers will be found by the acceptance run, by use, or\nby the story that makes it a promise.\n\nThe security section is reviewed by attacking it (`specs/adr/D-89`): for every guarantee of that\nsection, each reviewer looks for a concrete path, through the public entries the branch exposes, that\nobtains what the guarantee refuses. A path found is a bypass, marked as such in the finding with the\nguarantee it gets around. A bypass is blocking and holds the gate whatever its location; it never\ngoes to the registry, and after the last round it is put to the owner as a promise the code does not\nkeep. The answer to a round cannot class it elsewhere: a bypass it does not answer as fixed by a\ncommit it made, whether contested, registered or claimed fixed without one, holds the next round's\ngate as it stands. One it answers as fixed holds that gate too, until each reviewer of the next round\nhas replayed its path on the head of the branch and found it closed: a commit alone does not show\nwhat it fixes. A bypass the last round sends back to step 2 holds, in the same way, the gate of the\nround that follows the reopening. A review step relaunched after a block resumes at the round that\nfollows the last one answered, with the bypasses that answer left open or claimed fixed.\n\nA finding is located before it is handled. A defect the branch introduces or makes reachable belongs\nto it, even when the faulty line predates it; a defect it neither introduces nor makes reachable goes\nto the registry and does not hold the review back.\n\nThe answer to a round fixes what is blocking or to be fixed. A suggestion whose fix adds no behaviour\nis fixed in the round; a suggestion whose fix would add a refusal, a state or a mechanism goes to the\nregistry. A fix that adds a mechanism is designed before it is put in: where the state is born, who\nreads it, how many entries lead to it; removing a second entry is better than keeping it. A finding\nthat touches only text is fixed without being reviewed by fresh reviewers: the coordinator checks the\nfix against its finding.\n\nThe gate closes without a percentage. After the second round, what remains goes to the registry\n`specs/bugs/registry.yaml`, named as introduced by the branch when it is, except a promise the code\ndoes not keep, a bypass of the security section included, which is put to the owner: they decide\nbetween landing and a fix, which goes back through step 2 and one round on its diff.\n\nRecords do not move during review. The revision, timestamps and test count of a record are set when\nthe gate passes. A record lagging behind the reviewed revision is not a finding, any more than the\nsubject of a branch commit, which never reaches `main`.\n\n## Preflight and defects found along the way\n\nPreflight is `npm run check`. It is green before every step, and before every commit that touches\n`src/`, `test/`, `scripts/`, `bench/`, `contracts/`, `README.md`, `NOTICE`, `LICENSE`,\n`package.json`, `package-lock.json`, a `tsconfig*.json` or `biome.json`. A story under\n`specs/stories/` calls for `npm run lint:story-format`; an archive file calls for the check that\nreads it. A green Preflight holds as long as no file of the first list has changed: cite its\nrevision and time rather than run it again. Only a run under Node 24 counts.\n\nIn a run of the tool, only the tool runs Preflight: a session runs what its change touches — the tests\nconcerned, the typecheck, the lint — and hands its output back. After the red-green, the self-review\nand an answer to a review round, a red Preflight goes to a correction session with the failures the tool\nread, then runs again, at most twice before the step blocks. The base of a story and the squashed\nlanding have no session behind them: a red Preflight there blocks at once, and so does one after a\nrefactoring.\n\nA reproducible failure met along the way is a defect found, never background noise. It is fixed at\nonce, in its own commit, with its regression test, when the fix adds no behaviour; otherwise it gets\nan entry in `specs/bugs/registry.yaml` and its own cycle. It is recorded without a fix only if its\nreproduction fails after a good-faith attempt. \"Already there before\", \"unrelated to the session\",\n\"out of scope\" are not answers.\n\n## Git and commits\n\nOne branch per story or per fix cycle. Commit message: `<type>: <description>`, one line, in English,\nwhatever the language of the session or of the file changed; the types are `feat`, `fix`,\n`refactor`, `docs`, `test`, `chore`, `perf`, `ci`. The message states the resulting behaviour, never\nthe process that produced it: no work-package reference, no review round, no attribution, no\n`Co-Authored-By`. Nothing is pushed by an automaton; `gh` for every GitHub operation, never the API\ndirectly; no issue is created by an automaton.\n\n## What is written by hand, and what is observed\n\nBy hand: a product decision in `specs/adr/`, the order of the work in `specs/plan.yaml`, where\nthe owner marks `prete: oui` the epics that run without them, and, for every epic added to the plan,\nits dossier in `specs/epics/`, which the plan entry cites as `source:` and the session that writes\nits stories reads. The story and the registry entry are\nwritten by hand when the owner drives the story; in a run without them, a session writes them under\nthe rules of this file. Everything else is observed and recorded by the tool at the moment it\nobserves it: a red and its message, a Preflight and its revision, a review round and its findings,\nthe acceptance run and the owner's agreement, the landing. Evidence is written once, in the record of\nthe step that produced it; elsewhere it is cited. A copy drifts from the code it describes.\n\n## The cycle without the owner\n\n`npm run cycle -- suite` runs, one after the other, the epics that `specs/plan.yaml` marks\n`prete: oui`, in the order of the plan. For each: the next story of the plan is written by a session\nwhen none is listed to do, it is driven to landing as above, the plan marks it landed, and the epic\nbecomes `versé` when a session finds that its stories deliver its purpose. The run starts from `main`\nwith a clean tree and stops at the first block, saying why; it never pushes.\n`npm run cycle -- <story> auto` does the same for a single story.\n\nThree of the owner's answers are delegated, each an act recorded in the record:\n\n- **Agreement after the acceptance run.** A fresh session, which drove neither the acceptance run\n  nor the review, reads the story, the report of the acceptance run and the defects the branch\n  records in the registry, then decides `accepte` or `ecart` (`prompts/arbitrage.md`). For each\n  defect the branch records, it retains a severity and says why: the registry carries it, the record\n  keeps it beside the severity the branch wrote, and the tool stops, naming the defect, when one has\n  none. The tool reads the registry with a YAML reader, so it sees an entry whatever valid YAML form\n  it is written in, and stops, naming the registry, when the reader does not read it or the branch\n  adds an item that is not a defect with an identifier. The owner, when the story waits for them,\n  sees those defects with their severity in the question. A gap is a\n  written promise or a security guarantee not kept, a case the previous code stopped and the branch\n  lets through, or a defect of the branch that weakens a guarantee of the story; otherwise the\n  agreement carries a note naming what remains in the registry and what the acceptance run did not\n  exercise. A defect the arbitration finds and the registry does not carry, it records there and\n  commits; the tool stops if it touches another file or leaves the tree modified. A product question\n  the story does not settle is settled by the behaviour that stops. The decision is recorded with\n  `origine: automate`.\n- **The gap.** Whether the arbitration names it or the acceptance run reopens it, a session writes it\n  into the story (a scenario, the security sentence, a task whose red is checked in the code) and\n  commits it before red-green starts again: without a written promise, review cannot judge the fix.\n  The tool reads the story again, requires that it gained a scenario or a task and that the tree is\n  clean.\n- **A promise review did not get kept.** It goes back to red-green: it was not the owner's to waive.\n\n**Registry defects** are fixed too, at the right time (`D-73`): at the end of each epic, before the\nnext one, those of medium or high severity, whether the suite drove the epic or `cycle <story> auto`\nlanded its last story; at the end of the run, the low ones. A session picks the first open defect\nthat needs no product decision, writes its fix story under epic `e28` (which the plan never marks\nready) citing the registry entry, and the tool drives it through the six steps; at landing, the\nentry moves from the registry to `specs/bugs/registry-fixed.yaml`, marked fixed at the landed\nrevision. The defects the session sets aside because they need the owner are named at the end of the\nrun, with the reason, without stopping it. One phase fixes at most `CYCLE_495_DEFAUTS_MAX` defects\n(5 by default); `npm run cycle -- defauts [severity]` runs that phase alone.\n\nThe run stops, and hands back control, when a story has been sent back to red-green three times,\nwhen it has spent more than `CYCLE_495_PLAFOND_USD` ($80 by default), when a session does not return\nits output, or when a step blocks: a defect of the tool itself, a test that cannot be red, a red\nPreflight. Writing a story stops the same way on what it cannot write without choosing in the owner's\nplace (`bloque`, with the choice named), and an epic with no possible first story stays the owner's.\n\n## Refactorings\n\nA refactoring changes no behaviour: same events, same artifacts, same verdicts, same refusals, same\ncontracts, same texts meant for the owner or the model. It has no promise to review, no red to see,\nnothing to show at an acceptance run; so it does not go through the six steps, and takes the short\npath of `D-80`. What changes a behaviour, even slightly, is a story.\n\nRefactorings are written in `specs/reprises.md`, one section per refactoring, in the order they are\ndone. `npm run cycle -- reprises` drives them one after the other, from `main` and a clean tree:\n\n1. Preflight is green on `main`, after rebuilding `dist/`, and the tool reads its test count.\n2. A session does the refactoring on the branch `reprise-<id>` (`prompts/reprise.md`). It may set it\n   aside, with the reason, when the refactoring would change a behaviour or the code contradicts it:\n   the tool writes that into the list, commits, and moves to the next one.\n3. The tool checks, without trusting the session: at least one commit, a clean tree, the list\n   untouched, no assertion line of `test/` removed without coming back identical, Preflight green\n   after rebuilding `dist/`, and no fewer tests than before.\n4. A fresh session reviews the diff in a detached copy (`prompts/reprise-relecture.md`) and says\n   whether a behaviour changes or the diff overflows the refactoring. There is no second round.\n5. The branch reaches `main` in one commit, which marks the refactoring `versée` in the list; the\n   branch is deleted, the log under `~/.495/cycle/<id>/` keeps the transcripts.\n\nThe run stops, and hands back control, at the first check that fails or at a review that sees a\nchange: the branch stays checked out so it can be read, `main` and the list do not move. To resume,\ngo back to `main` (`git checkout main`): the next run starts the same refactoring from a fresh branch,\nor it is set aside by hand in the list with the reason. When review refuses what the refactoring\nitself asks for, running again replays the same refusal: reduce the refactoring to its constant part,\nsaying in `Limite` what it does not do, and the rest goes to the registry or to a story.\n`CYCLE_495_REPRISES_MAX` caps the number of refactorings in one run. Nothing is pushed.\n\n## The tool\n\n`npm run cycle -- <story>` drives the remaining steps, in order, until a step needs the owner or\nblocks; `npm run cycle -- <story> etat` says where it stands;\n`npm run cycle -- <story> suivre` follows, from another terminal, the story that is running;\n`npm run cycle -- suite` runs the epics marked ready, without the owner;\n`npm run cycle -- defauts [severity]` fixes the open defects of the registry, without running an epic;\n`npm run cycle -- reprises` drives the behaviour-preserving refactorings of `specs/reprises.md`;\n`npm run cycle -- <story> auto` drives a single story the same way;\n`npm run cycle -- <story> accepte [note]` records the agreement after the acceptance run;\n`npm run cycle -- <story> ecart \"<what is missing>\"` sends it back to red-green for the named gap,\nwith a single review round on its diff. The record lives under `~/.495/cycle/<story>/` during the\nstory (`CYCLE_495_DIR` moves it).\n\nWhile it runs, the command shows in the terminal where the story stands: each step opens with its\nplace among the six, with the time and cost already spent; each session prints one line per agent\ntext, tool call, commit, test total or failed call, prefixed with its name when two reviewers run\ntogether; each check says when it starts and how it ends; each step closes on its outcome, duration,\ncost and commits. The terminal title names the current step and how long nothing has been printed. A\nsound and a notification say that a step waits for the owner, that it blocks or that the story has\nlanded (`CYCLE_495_SON` changes the sound). The same lines go to `en-direct.log`, in the story's\ndirectory, which `npm run cycle -- <story> suivre` follows from another terminal until Ctrl-C; a new\nrun empties that file. Nothing in this display is evidence: the log and the kept transcripts are.\n\nThe tool depends on 495 and never the reverse. It takes from the kernel the object store, where the\ntranscripts, check outputs and reviewer reports go; the check executor and its report readers,\nthrough which Preflight, each task's command and the replay of each red pass; and the shape of\nevidence. Checks run unconfined, and each piece of evidence says so: this repository's suite\nqualifies Seatbelt itself, and sandboxes do not nest.\n\nThe steps that need a model run in Claude Code sessions, one per step, started with no background\ntask and no possible question, with a structured output the tool reads: red-green, self-review, the\ntwo reviewers of each round in parallel in their detached trees, the answer, the acceptance run, the\nlanding message. Their prompts are in `prompts/`. The story itself is written with the owner, in an\nordinary session.\n\nWhat the tool checks itself, without trusting the session: each test-only commit of the pass,\nreplayed in a detached tree, fails on a test it reads; each task command passes at the branch head;\nPreflight is green at the cited revision; review stops at two rounds; the branch reaches `main` in\none commit; both reference campaigns are green at the head the agreement accepts, when the branch\nrequires them, which it judges from the base it recorded when the story's branch started; no session\nwrites the story's record, and what one adds or removes there while it runs is taken back out and\nblocks its step; the record is exported at landing. A session that forges what the tool reads back\nbehind its back — the record, the repository's git configuration, a detached process writing after\nthese checks — stays outside them while the sessions run unconfined.\n\nBefore the first review round, the tool mutates the lines the branch introduces in the TypeScript\nsources of `src/`, `cycle/src/` and `scripts/`, with Stryker and the test files the tasks' commands\nname, in a detached tree removed afterwards; it keeps the report in the record and gives the reviewers\nthe mutants that survive (`D-89`). A mutation that fails or runs past its budget\n(`CYCLE_495_MUTATION_MIN`, 30 minutes by default) does not hold the round back: the reviewers are told\nit did not complete, and why.\n\nWhat is still missing: 495's confined Pi worker in place of the Claude Code sessions.\n","numLines":357,"startLine":1,"totalLines":357}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_013dvkVNbPTBNWKe5hVk3EYS","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
